\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Changing the phase of one coupling}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Multiply one complex coupling by a unit scalar.
Its adjoint acquires the inverse scalar, so composing the two
removes that phase. What can two such composites on the same
space retain about the relative position of their eigenspaces?
The scalar action on a single [Yukawa map](fdip-011M) already
distinguishes a map from its composite with its adjoint.}
